Diane looked at Miriam. The medium’s voice carried no irritation. She looked from Evan’s enlarged portrait to the program beneath the glass and pressed her lips together.

Evan turned the camera toward the stage. “Marian Bell directed here. Another Marian Bell wrote plays and worked in vaudeville. Miriam sensed her before we opened the case.”

“We entered facing the case,” Abby said. “The name was legible from the aisle.”

“You assume she read it.”

“I record that she could.”

The assistant stood beside the first row, the blanket folded against her hip. “Maybe the school Marian Bell admired the playwright.”

Diane pressed the marker.

Evan looked at her. “That could explain why the same spirit uses both histories.”

“Or it could combine two people because their names match,” Diane said.

Miriam raised one hand. “Please stop building her.”

No one answered.

The vent continued above the doors. The piano key held level beneath its cover. The red seats remained fixed in their rows. After the search, the stillness required more attention than the earlier noises.

Miriam faced the stage-left wing.

“If a woman is present, I perceive affection for the room and resistance to departure. I lack a verified name. I lack evidence that she directed here, wrote plays, entered this building, or died in connection with it.”

Evan lowered his phone.

“She remains after the cleansing?” he asked.

“The basement ritual addressed the basement group and any listener there. My present impression concerns this room.”

“So the school is not clear.”

Miriam considered the stage. “I regard the harmful pressure downstairs as resolved within my practice. I perceive another presence here that appears peaceful and chooses to remain.”

Evan looked into his camera. “The cleansing removed the dangerous activity while leaving a protective theater spirit.”

Diane stepped into his frame. “That is a new claim.”

“It summarizes what she said.”

“It changes peaceful to protective, impression to spirit, and basement session to school cleansing.”

He shifted the camera around her.

Abby said, “Keep both statements.”

Diane pointed to the display case. “Also keep the program, every search term, every rejected result, and the lack of a school connection for the playwright.”

Miriam’s assistant touched the edge of a seat. “Could we ask for a sign from the woman?”

“No provocation,” Diane said.

“A sign could be harmless.”

“The group has just supplied a name, occupation, location, motive, and historical identity. Any sound now enters that context.”

Miriam moved beside the assistant. “We can hold silence without requesting performance.”

Diane nodded to Abby.

“Continuous sources,” Abby said. “Forty-five seconds. Fixed positions.”

They arranged themselves across the front of the auditorium. Abby placed her camera on the center aisle tripod. Evan set his beside it after Diane required the display case, stage-left wing, and front seats within the same wide frame. Miriam stood at the aisle end of the first row. Her assistant remained two seats away. Diane placed her recorder on the stage edge, microphone facing the room, then returned to the aisle.

Abby stated the time.

“No questions,” Diane said. “No proposed name. Listen for ordinary sources first.”

The hold began.

At six seconds, the vent above the lobby doors clicked and changed speed.

At eleven, the assistant’s coat sleeve brushed the blanket.

At nineteen, the sticking piano key rose another fraction and tapped beneath the canvas.

At twenty-seven, a seat spring creaked near the rear section. Abby kept the wide frame fixed.

At thirty-four, Evan drew breath and closed his mouth when Diane raised one finger.

At forty-one, Miriam turned her head toward stage left.

Abby ended the hold at forty-five.

“What changed?” Diane asked.

Miriam kept her attention on the wing. “I perceive the impression farther backstage.”

“Physical observation?”

“A cooling sensation across my right hand and the sense of someone withdrawing.”

Diane checked the nearest wall thermometer. “Sixty-eight degrees. Same as entry.”

The assistant said, “I felt calmer.”

Abby marked both reports.

Evan looked toward the display case. “Marian accepts that we know who she is.”

Miriam faced him. “I asked you to stop building her.”

He lowered the camera.

Diane returned to the stage and retrieved her recorder without playing it. The red light remained steady. She stated the file number and locked the controls.

Evan packed his tripod. “We cleared what needed clearing. Whatever stays here belongs here.”

“No one established either sentence,” Diane said.

“The basement changed. The meter returned. Miriam says the woman is peaceful.”

“Miriam says she perceives less basement pressure and a separate auditorium impression. The meter returned after reseating. The woman lacks a verified identity.”

He looked across the empty red seats. “You can write it that way. People will still understand what happened.”

Diane slid the recorder into its padded pouch. “They will understand what survives the edit.”

Miriam’s assistant lifted the knitted hat from beneath the seat and placed it on the cushion for the morning staff. The seat snapped upright when her hand came away.

The group turned toward the doors.

On the recorder inside Diane’s pouch, the input level had risen once during the silent hold, for less than a second, while no participant spoke and the fixed cameras showed only minor movement.

## Scene 9 — The Final Source

By eight the next morning, Abby had aligned every recording source to the stairwell clock.

The media room smelled of warm plastic, dust, and the coffee Evan had left untouched beside the editing console. Four monitors cast the same basement corridor across the room from different angles. Colored markers divided the timeline: camera starts, device failures, participant movements, Miriam’s impressions, Diane’s interventions, the ritual, and the auditorium hold. Each screen preserved a different edge of the night; none preserved the whole room.

Diane stood behind Abby with the facilities log open on a clipboard. Evan occupied the chair nearest the main screen. Miriam sat at the end of the worktable with her folded ritual card beside both hands. Her assistant had gone home after signing a release that barred public use of her name, face, family history, and emotional reactions.

Abby enlarged the first marker.

“Introduction leak,” she said. “Five separate exposures before the formal start.”

She played the service-vestibule audio. Evan’s voice carried through the open stair door: Diane’s name, Diane’s job, the restricted corridor, the prior documentary. A minute later the assistant used the name while asking where coats belonged. Miriam answered her without repeating it. In the first basement room, Evan called to Diane from the crawlspace entrance. Before the fire-door sequence, the assistant supplied the name again.

Abby stopped playback.

Miriam studied the time map. “I heard it.”

Evan rubbed one thumb along the edge of his camera card case. “The approximation still came from her. Deena before Diane.”

“After three clear exposures and one partial exposure,” Diane said.

“An approximation can still matter.”

“It can matter without qualifying as private information.”

Abby entered the conclusion in the review column: NAME EXPOSURE CONFIRMED. INDEPENDENT IDENTIFICATION UNSUPPORTED.

Evan read it and leaned back.

Diane kept her pen on the clipboard. “Next.”

Abby cleared the minor image findings first. The black crawlspace photograph remained an original failed frame, but Evan’s finger crossed the lens edge, the phone angle changed, and autofocus and exposure data were unavailable after handling and export. The pale shape on Abby’s camera stayed near the lens, with blanket fibers, moisture, and changing air movement available.





She moved to the devices. The meter had opened outside coverage, carried residue at one contact, failed on the first post-ritual press, and returned only after the replacement batteries were pressed and reseated. The flashlight contained torn wrapper material against a contact.

No item justified a presence claim. Each remained in the chronology because its timing influenced how the group interpreted the room. Miriam insisted the assistant’s relief preceded the meter’s recovery. Diane allowed that distinction: the ritual could retain spiritual meaning in Miriam’s interview, while the record supported reported relief and unresolved equipment behavior.

The classroom-storage room required longer.

Abby placed Miriam’s broad statements on the left side of the screen. Young. Distressed. Trouble with words or reception. Social pain. A fond association with a familiar cartoon. On the right, she placed the contributions that followed: Evan’s school interpretation, the assistant’s bullying association, the anonymous classroom exercise, the private joke, the family disclosure, and the identification of Miriam’s uncle as the helper.

Abby drew arrows between the columns until the broad statements and later contributions formed a biography on the screen.

Miriam studied the map. “I still believe someone received mercy there.”

Miriam’s fingers rested flat beside the ritual card. No ring or bracelet touched the table.

“Your belief can remain in your interview,” Diane said. “The documentary cannot identify a student, describe a real death, or state that the note belonged to a dead person.”

“The note moved the group,” Evan said.

“The note came from a classroom exercise about private worries. No name, date, or link to a death appears on it.”

“It matched the impression.”

“After the group supplied the category.”

Miriam studied the two columns. “Use my words from last night. We joined details that may belong to different lives.”

Evan turned away from the monitors.

Abby advanced to the fire-door event.

In the synchronized footage, the group could see each participant’s position and the open area beside the frame. Evan’s shot omitted the lower hinge; Abby’s omitted the bottom edge. The supply fan changed speed. The closer arm flexed. The door released from the wall stop, crossed roughly half the opening, rebounded, and rattled. No visible hand touched the slab. No camera covered every mechanical point or every participant’s lower body.

The low syllable beneath the rattle appeared on Abby’s track and faintly on Evan’s. Diane’s recorder, farther from the door, contained mostly the closer impact and Evan’s breath.

Abby played the isolated segment once without prompting.

Miriam wrote on a clean slip. Evan wrote on another. Diane left hers blank. Abby folded each response before anyone spoke.

Miriam read first. “Down.”

Evan read, “Diane.”

Abby opened her own. “Vowel onset under impact. No reliable word.”

Diane wrote the three responses into the log.

“The door remains the strongest physical event,” Evan said.

“It also remains incompletely observed,” Diane said.

“You treat those as equal.”

“They belong in the same sentence.”

The personal-detail sequence occupied another set of arrows. Bakery box visible in Diane’s car. Product name printed on its side. Miriam’s path past the vehicle. Plant material caught in Diane’s cuff. School keys and car fob visible during the hardware inspection. Evan’s crash narrative supplied before Miriam connected the car image to Diane.

Miriam watched each exposure appear in order.

“I offered broad images,” she said. “The people in the room made them exact.”

Evan’s chair creaked when he shifted. “Two broad images still landed on the same person.”

“Because everyone had already turned toward her,” Miriam said.

Diane marked the sentence for possible inclusion.

The auditorium sequence took six minutes to reconstruct and nearly an hour to discuss.

